Status
  A release candidate for pilot use and comment. Not a final MLCommons standard, certification
  scheme or endorsement. Conformance means a Profile is well-formed, consistent, honestly labelled
  and signed; it does not mean its claims are true or the agent is safe.

Source of the concepts
  Agent Reliability Profiles in Financial Services, arXiv:2610.04123 (October 2026).
  https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.04123

Third-party sources
  - The control catalogue (spec/vocab/control-catalogue.json, 55 controls in six layers) is the
    Working Group's own wording (preprint Appendix B.4). It is designed to be crosswalked to OWASP,
    NIST and other public frameworks; those frameworks are referred to, not copied.
  - spec/interop/assert-and-acs.md describes how Profiles relate to Microsoft's ASSERT project
    (MIT licence) and to agent control specifications. It refers to, and does not include,
    their code or text.
  - The browser tools use only the browser's built-in cryptography (Ed25519 via Web Crypto where
    available) and include no third-party libraries.

Fictional data
  Every institution, deployment, person, model, endpoint and key in examples/, templates/examples/,
  skill-trial/ and regulator/sample-feed/ is fictional, and names carry "(fictional)". Any
  resemblance to a real institution is unintended; report it and it will be renamed. Keys marked
  DEMO-ONLY are public and must never sign a real Profile. Some evidence hashes are placeholders.
  The sample feed contains deliberately planted problems, listed in README.md
  ("Sample and demo data"). The Level 2 example
  (examples/l2-open-banking.json) is an illustrative Working Group example, not a model for the
  public pilot, which is Level 1 only (README, "Public pilot").
